A JOIN query is slow:
SELECT * FROM big_table a JOIN small_table b ON a.id = b.a_id;

Big_table has 10M rows, small_table has 100 rows. What join order is optimal?

A subquery is correlated and runs slowly:
SELECT name FROM users u WHERE salary > (SELECT AVG(salary) FROM users WHERE department = u.department);

How would you optimize?

Queries on a frequently accessed table are slow despite indexes. Cache at the database level is fragmented. What helps?
ANALYZE TABLE users;
OR
VACUUM FULL users;

A UNION query combines results from two tables with different schemas:
SELECT id, name FROM users UNION SELECT id, title FROM products;

Why might this be slow?
